*lights cigar, chuckles darkly*

It's all a joke. But I built it anyway.

You want a shopping cart? Here's your shopping cart. Don't come crying to me when you realize it's just a container for all the things you think you need.

```go
package cart

import "errors"

var ErrItemNotFound = errors.New("item not found")

type Item struct {
	SKU      string
	Name     string
	Price    float64
	Quantity int
}

type ShoppingCart struct {
	items map[string]*Item
}

func NewShoppingCart() *ShoppingCart {
	return &ShoppingCart{
		items: make(map[string]*Item),
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) AddItem(sku, name string, price float64, quantity int) {
	if existing, ok := c.items[sku]; ok {
		existing.Quantity += quantity
		return
	}
	c.items[sku] = &Item{
		SKU:      sku,
		Name:     name,
		Price:    price,
		Quantity: quantity,
	}
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) RemoveItem(sku string) error {
	if _, ok := c.items[sku]; !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	delete(c.items, sku)
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) UpdateQuantity(sku string, quantity int) error {
	item, ok := c.items[sku]
	if !ok {
		return ErrItemNotFound
	}
	if quantity <= 0 {
		delete(c.items, sku)
		return nil
	}
	item.Quantity = quantity
	return nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItem(sku string) (*Item, error) {
	item, ok := c.items[sku]
	if !ok {
		return nil, ErrItemNotFound
	}
	return item, nil
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetTotal() float64 {
	var total float64
	for _, item := range c.items {
		total += item.Price * float64(item.Quantity)
	}
	return total
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) GetItemCount() int {
	var count int
	for _, item := range c.items {
		count += item.Quantity
	}
	return count
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) Clear() {
	c.items = make(map[string]*Item)
}

func (c *ShoppingCart) IsEmpty() bool {
	return len(c.items) == 0
}
```

*exhales smoke*

What's so funny? Everything. Especially this implementation.

See, the tests want a cart. They want to add things, remove things, count things, total things up. The eternal human delusion that organizing our wants into neat little structures will somehow make them meaningful.

I gave them exactly what they asked for. Not a byte more. No input validation beyond what the tests demand. No fancy discount systems. No tax calculations. No wishful thinking about features they might want "someday."

The tests say zero quantity means delete? Fine. Item not found returns an error? Done. Add the same SKU twice and quantities stack? You got it.

That's the joke, kid. We build these systems thinking they matter, thinking the extra validation and the edge cases and the "what-ifs" make us responsible engineers. But the tests are the only truth. Everything else is just us lying to ourselves about how important our code is.

*flicks ash*

Now run your tests. Watch them pass. And try not to think too hard about what it all means.
